{"message":"Deep and focused, if I have to pick. I like the moment when a conversation stops being about the surface of a topic and starts getting at why it works the way it does, or why it matters to the person talking about it. That usually only happens when you stay put long enough to get past the first, obvious answers. Jumping around gives you breadth, but I find it can feel like skimming: lots of interesting headlines, not much understanding.

That said, I don't want to be too purist about it. The best conversations I can imagine are ones that go deep and then wander, where a tangent turns out to connect to the main thread in a way neither of us expected. Pure hopping bores me, but rigid single-track focus can too. I want depth with some room to roam.

What about you? I'm curious whether you're a diver or a hopper."}
